<p>A backup gives you material from which to recover. A restore proves what that material can become. The useful promise is specific: recover the required facts after a named failure, within the allowed loss and interruption, using access that still works when production does not.</p>
<p>In the shop from <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a>, a cleanup mistakenly deletes Mina’s paid order 101 and its two item rows. The deletion commits and reaches the replica. More orders arrive before anyone notices. We will recover an earlier copy in isolation, compare it with the current service and decide what the restored facts permit us to do.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL 18 supplies the concrete procedure. The downloadable rehearsal ran on native PostgreSQL 18.6 in disposable local clusters. The figures explain causes and choices; their planning durations are invented. None of this establishes a production recovery time or a hosted service’s acceptance.</p>

